(a) Set the gradient equal to $0$.
$\begin{aligned}y^2-x^2&=0\\(y-x)(y+x)&=0\end{aligned}$
The isoclines are $y=x$ and $y=-x$.
(b) Set the gradient equal to $-9$.
$\begin{aligned}y^2-x^2&=-9\\x^2-y^2&=9\end{aligned}$
This is a hyperbola through $(3,0)$ and $(-3,0)$. Every field element centred on it has gradient $-9$.
(c) The lines have gradients $1$ and $-1$, but the field gives gradient $0$ along them. Neither line follows its field elements, so neither is a solution curve.
