(a) Substitute the line into the right-hand side.
$\begin{aligned}-2x-y&=-2x-(2-2x)\\&=-2\end{aligned}$
The field gradient is constant along the line, so it is an isocline. The line's own derivative is also $-2$, so it is a solution curve.
(b) At $(0,3)$ the gradient is $-2(0)-3=-3$, so the curve initially falls.
Its vertical separation from the straight solution is
$\begin{aligned}y-(2-2x)&=e^{-x}\\&>0.\end{aligned}$
It stays above the line and never meets it. As $x$ increases, $e^{-x}$ approaches $0$, so $y=2-2x$ is an oblique asymptote.
